WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.529
For every fan theory The Last Jedi Force-choked
into oblivion, it seems like two new questions

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:07.039
pop up in their place.

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:11.630
New details and references hidden in the movie
are still emerging, and some of them may have

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.420
more meaning than you realize.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.920
Here are some of the crazy references you
missed in The Last Jedi.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.700
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:19.700
Blaster damage

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.440
In Return of the Jedi, Luke got smacked by
a blaster bolt while he was trying to escape

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.780
Jabba's boat over the sarlacc pit.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.170
Luckily, that laser landed on Luke's fake
hand, and Luke went right back to almost kicking

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:31.720
the bad guys into the sand.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.930
In The Last Jedi, old, grumpy Luke is still
one hand down, but if you look closely, especially

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:41.120
at the part where Rey hands him his old lightsaber,
you can see faint scorch marks on the back

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.160
of his hand, a reminder of young Skywalker's
glory days.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.270
No hand Luke

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:50.770
Speaking of Luke's fake hand, director Rian
Johnson himself pointed out an inconsistency

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.800
that may reveal more for the future of the
franchise.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:58.460
After Force-projecting himself to Crait to
deal with Kylo Ren, Luke vanishes from his

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.960
rock, by all accounts becoming one with the
Force.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.820
However, there was one detail missing from
that scene.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:04.820
As Johnson put it,

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.810
"We were in the other room saying, 'A steel
hand should clunk to the ground.'"

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.280
Since Luke's prosthetic hand wasn't actually
a part of him, it should have been left behind,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.299
just like his Jedi robe.

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:17.110
Does that mean there's more to Luke's story?

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:21.520
Probably not, but you can bet it'll spark
a new round of theories before the next movie.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.520
Canine cameo

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.520
There was a lot going on during the Canto
Bight scenes, so you probably missed one particular

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.510
cameo–Carrie Fisher's dog, Gary.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:33.890
Well, even if you saw the alien dog onscreen,
you may not have recognized it as anything

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.869
special, because for one, it was just a dog,
and for two, it was only onscreen for a second.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:42.700
According to the dog's official Twitter account,
one of the aliens in the casino was holding

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.840
a dog based on Fisher's own canine.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.210
Did it mean anything to the story?

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:47.210
Nope!

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:51.231
But it was a nice nod to the late Star Wars
legend and a cool source of inspiration, if

00:01:51.231 --> 00:01:52.750
nothing else.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.030
Hidden knowledge

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.119
One of the big moments in The Last Jedi came
when Yoda Force-called a lightning bolt down

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:01.649
to scorch the sacred tree holding all those
ancient Jedi books.

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:04.310
Well, as usual, Yoda had the last laugh.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.280
"Take you to him I will.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.280
Hahah!"

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.239
At the end of the film, Finn opens a drawer
in the Millennium Falcon holding all the Jedi

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:14.040
texts that from the sacred tree.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.909
It gives a lot more meaning to Yoda's final
line to Luke:

00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:20.419
"That library contained nothing that the girl
Rey does not already possess."

00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:24.569
It was a blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment,
but rest assured that the Jedi wisdom is safe

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:27.849
and secure for a new generation of Force users.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:29.299
Costume secrets

00:02:29.299 --> 00:02:33.019
Although the film never draws attention to
them, the production team on The Last Jedi

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.999
put a ton of detail into the characters' costumes.

00:02:35.999 --> 00:02:40.290
For example, Vice Admiral Holdo's bracelets
are a map of the constellations seen from

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:41.290
her homeworld.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.209
It's a crazy detail that nobody would ever
notice, and it's just the beginning.

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.529
Poe Dameron wears a necklace that's usually
covered up by the collar of his jacket, but

00:02:49.529 --> 00:02:51.689
a few scenes show the chain around his neck.

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:55.639
What they don't show is the ring looped into
the chain, which is his deceased mother's

00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:56.769
wedding ring.

00:02:56.769 --> 00:02:59.579
Similarly, Rose sports a secret ring of her
own.

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:03.860
When Rose and Finn are on Canto Bight, there's
a quick moment where Rose flashes a Resistance

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:04.909
symbol on her ring.

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:08.700
As it turns out, that's an antique left over
from the Galactic Civil War, when they were

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.790
worn by Imperial Senators who would flash
the secret symbol to other Rebels to prove

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.760
their allegiance.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:15.760
Dwartii glyphs

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.669
Supreme Leader Snoke has come and gone, with
no new clues to his origins.

00:03:19.669 --> 00:03:24.030
Well, except for one…which, honestly, kind
of lends some credence to all those "Snoke

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.480
is Darth Plagueis" theories.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.540
For those who don't know, Darth Plagueis was
the master of Darth Sidious, until Sidious

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.120
struck down Plagueis during his rise to power.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.770
You could only see it clearly in two or three
shots, but Snoke was wearing a ring in The

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:40.219
Last Jedi—located on his left hand, it had
a gold base with a black stone rising out

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:41.219
of it.

00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:45.109
According to The Last Jedi: The Visual Dictionary,
the gold section is inscribed with "glyphs

00:03:45.109 --> 00:03:46.109
of the Dwartii."

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:50.540
Dwartii is a planet that was home to the Sages
of Dwartii, four mysterious philosophers who

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.469
were apparently revered by the Sith, judging
by the statues of them in Palpatine's office

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:56.969
in Revenge of the Sith and Attack of the Clones.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:01.449
The novel Darth Plagueis also suggests that
one of the statues belonged to Plagueis.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:05.739
It isn't exactly a strong connection to Snoke,
but the markings on Snoke's ring are literally

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:08.790
one of the handful of times Dwartii has shown
up in Star Wars.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.129
While it's doubtful that Snoke really is Darth
Plagueis, the ring suggests there may still

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:15.629
be some connection between the two.

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:17.340
Praetorian symmetry

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.750
Like a lot of other characters in The Last
Jedi, Snoke's personal bodyguards, the Praetorian

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.180
Guard, were introduced, then killed off without
much explanation.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:30.490
Fortunately, these faceless red warriors made
the most of their time onscreen and gave audiences

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.389
one of the best fights of the franchise.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:37.030
Amidst all the action, you may have missed
a peculiar detail about the Praetorian Guards–although

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.180
there are eight of them, they're organized
into four pairs, with each pair armed with

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.440
identical weapons.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.200
The Visual Dictionary explains that this was
done to emphasize the symmetry of the First

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.590
Order philosophy: "What is past is future."

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.590
Name check

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:55.050
If you missed Joseph Gordon-Levitt's cameo
in The Last Jedi, you aren't alone–nobody

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.169
ever said his character's name during the
movie.

00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:01.580
In short, he voiced the alien on Canto Bight
who led the police to Finn and Rose, and his

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:02.580
name was Slowen Lo.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:06.539
That name is part of an ongoing joke from
the filmmakers to give certain characters

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:09.180
names pulled come from Beastie Boys songs,
of all things.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.719
Slowen Lo comes from the song "Slow and Low."

00:05:11.719 --> 00:05:17.100
The Force Awakens has Ello Asty from the song
"Hello Nasty", Roodown from "Root Down," and

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.879
Brasmon Kee from "Brass Monkey."

00:05:18.879 --> 00:05:22.400
Have the Star Wars writers finally run out
of ideas for names?

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.539
Not even close–that happened way back whenever
Salacious B. Crumb showed up.

00:05:26.539 --> 00:05:28.150
Prime Jedi

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:31.889
If you've ever wondered who the first Jedi
was, you're not alone, and The Last Jedi may

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:32.889
have an answer.

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:37.290
On the floor of the Jedi temple on Luke's
island is a mosaic of the Prime Jedi, the

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.039
first guy to be part of the Jedi Order.

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:43.250
It shows up in several scenes during the movie,
most clearly when Luke is telling Rey his

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:46.310
first abridged version of the "We Need to
Talk About Kylo" story.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.259
So who is the Prime Jedi?

00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:50.439
Does he have a name?

00:05:50.439 --> 00:05:53.460
Unfortunately, the movie doesn't have any
explanations there.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:54.460
Don't Join

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:57.780
Benicio del Toro's character, D.J., is definitely
an enigma.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.039
He showed up, got Finn and Rose onto Snoke's
ship, then sold out the Resistance and skipped

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:03.430
out all the richer for it.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:07.449
But for those paying close attention, his
moral ambiguity was practically written on

00:06:07.449 --> 00:06:08.979
his forehead from the beginning.

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:12.250
You may have caught the metal plate on the
right side of D.J.'s headgear, but unless

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:16.190
you paused the movie in the theaters, you
probably missed the inscription on it: Don't

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:17.190
Join.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.910
Yup, that's what he told Finn, and it's D.J.'s
whole philosophy, up to and including his

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:23.389
name–D.J., a.k.a.

00:06:23.389 --> 00:06:25.169
Don't Join.

00:06:25.169 --> 00:06:26.189
Crusader crystal

00:06:26.189 --> 00:06:29.759
Near the beginning of the film, the camera
moves through Luke's hut and rests on a few

00:06:29.759 --> 00:06:31.010
of his belongings.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.500
Some fans were quick to notice that a necklace
hanging in the hut seemed to contain a red

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.009
kyber crystal–the crystal used to power
lightsabers.

00:06:38.009 --> 00:06:41.419
Turns out, that's exactly what it is, but
the story doesn't end there.

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:45.909
Going back to the film's visual dictionary,
it's described as a fragmented Sith lightsaber

00:06:45.909 --> 00:06:49.389
crystal, but the necklace itself is a Jedi
Crusader pendant.

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:53.330
The Jedi Crusaders were first mentioned in
Star Wars: Knights of the Old Republic—they

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:58.479
were also known as the Revanchists, named
after their leader Revan, a Jedi who succumbed

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:00.639
to the dark side and became Darth Revan.

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:04.569
That's significant, because Knights of the
Old Republic was purged from the official

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:05.569
canon.

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:08.310
Although there have been hints that the game
was creeping its way back into canon, this

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.879
may be the biggest indicator of that yet.

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:15.690
Does this tiny detail in The Last Jedi reinstate
Revan as a canonical character?

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.120
Fans can only hope.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.120
Thanks for watching!

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.979
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:20.979 --> 00:07:23.009
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

